"例えば \\(K_5^*\\) は図4に示すように赤の辺と青の辺に分割することでそれぞれ planar graph となる。また，一般の \\(K_n^*\\) についても同様の分割によって biplanar graph であることを示すことができる。 (https://www.math.sci.hokudai.ac.jp/~wakate/mcyr/2024/pdf/kitano_sota.pdf)\nciteturn12search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; 以上から biplanar graph 全体の族について odd chromatic number の上限は存在しない。\n例えば \\(K_5^*\\) は図4に示すように赤の辺と青の辺に分割することでそれぞれ planar graph となる。また，一般の \\(K_n^*\\) についても同様の分割によって biplanar graph であることを示すことができる。\n\n以上から biplanar graph 全体の族について odd chromatic number の上限は存在しない。一方でこの族の chromatic number の上限は12以下であることが分かっている。そこで以下の問題を考える。\n\n**問題 3.4.** Biplanar graph 全体の族にどのような条件を課せば odd chromatic number の上限が12以下になるか。\n\nこの疑問に対して本稿では以下を主定理として示す。この定理は筆者のオリジナルの結果である。\n\n**定理 4.8.** 任意の biplanar graph \\(G\\) について，\\(\\delta(G)\\geq 3\\)，\\(\\operatorname{gir}(G)\\geq 6\\) ならば \\(\\chi_o(G)\\leq 12\\)。\n\nこの定理によって少なくとも \\(\\delta(G)\\geq 3\\)，\\(\\operatorname{gir}(G)\\geq 6\\) という条件を課せば \\(\\chi_o(G)\\leq 12\\) であることは示されたが，これ以上に条件を緩めることができるかについては分かっていない。\n\n## 4　主定理の証明\n\n主定理の証明には二つの補題と放電法を用いる。準備として odd \\(c^+\\)-critical graph を定義する。\n\n**定義 4.1.**（Odd \\(c^+\\)-critical graph）Graph \\(G\\) が \\(\\chi_o(G)\\geq c\\) かつ任意の proper subgraph \\(H\\) に対して \\(\\chi_o(H)<c\\) を満たすとき，\\(G\\) を odd \\(c^+\\)-critical graph と呼び，odd \\(c^+\\)-critical graph の全体を \\(\\mathcal{S}_c\\) と書く。\n\nそもそも一般に graph \\(G\\) と subgraph \\(H\\) に対して \\(\\chi_o(H)\\leq\\chi_o(G)\\) が成り立つとは限らないが，定理を証明する際には odd \\(c^+\\)-critical graph という極小性を持った graph を考えることは有用である。\n\n**例 4.2.**（Odd \\(c^+\\)-critical graph の例）図5の \\(C_5\\) は odd \\(5^+\\)-critical graph である。実際，\\(\\chi_o(C_5)=5\\) であるが，任意の proper subgraph \\(H\\) について \\(\\chi_o(H)<5\\) となる。\n\n一つ目の補題は odd \\(c^+\\)-critical の基本的な構造について述べたものである。--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInnov. Graph Theory 1, 2024, pp. 21–32 (https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/igt.2.pdf)\nciteturn12search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Conjecture 1.4 (Strong Nine Dragon Tree Conjecture [9]). — Let G ... sparse if for every subgraph H of G we have\nTHE OVERFULL NINE DRAGON TREE CONJECTURE IS TRUE\n23\nTheorem 1.3 ([9]). — For any positive integers k and d there are ar-\nbitrarily large simple graphs G and a set S = {e1, . . . , ed+1} ⊆E(G) of\nd + 1 edges such that γ(G −S) = k +\nd\nk+d+1 and G does not decompose\ninto k + 1 forests where one of the forests has maximum degree d.\nThus, it looks like the story is essentially complete. However, two possible\ngeneralizations were proposed. The more famous one is the so-called Strong\nNine Dragon Tree Conjecture:\nConjecture 1.4 (Strong Nine Dragon Tree Conjecture [9]). — Let G\nbe a graph. Let k and d be positive integers. If γ(G) ⩽k +\nd\nk+d+1, then\nthere is a decomposition into k + 1 forests, where one of the forests has\nevery connected component having at most d edges.\nThis conjecture has attracted some amount of attention - see for example\n[8, 9, 11, 5, 6], and still remains fairly wide open. However, there is a dif-\nferent way to generalize the Nine Dragon Tree Theorem, which is the focus\nof this paper. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Nine Dragon Tree Conjectures (https://openscience.ub.uni-mainz.de/bitstreams/db2c6b84-8332-44dd-a125-635d09470cc1/download)\nciteturn12search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; 3.7 The Strong Nine Dragon Tree Conjecture for ... In this section, we prove Theorem 1.14, showing that (k, d)-sparse graphs with\n3.7 The Strong Nine Dragon Tree Conjecture for\nd ≤2(k + 1)\nIn this section, we prove Theorem 1.14, showing that (k, d)-sparse graphs with\nγ(G) ≤k + 1 are (k, d)-decomposable for d ≤2(k + 1), building on Section 3.3 and\nSection 3.5.\nRecall that the Strong Nine Dragon Tree Conjecture for d ≤k +1 was proven in [38]\nby the author and Moore. Furthermore, Theorem 1.15 reproves this case. Thus, for\nthe rest of the section, we let k + 1 < d ≤2(k + 1) (and we still let k ≥1 due to 3.3.1).\nIn particular, d ≥3. Thus, we have ℓ= 1, so that small components either have\nzero or one edge. The techniques we use for this case build on the same ideas from\n[38] and from the previous section: we bound the number of small children as in\nLemma 3.6.8. For d ≤k + 1, where ℓ= 0, this bound suffices to prove the Strong\nNine Dragon Tree Conjecture. But this is not the case if d > k + 1, where ℓ= 1. For\nexample, a non-small component K with e(K) = d can have k children with zero\nedges and k children with one edge. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Nine Dragon Tree Conjectures (https://openscience.ub.uni-mainz.de/server/api/core/bitstreams/db2c6b84-8332-44dd-a125-635d09470cc1/content)\nciteturn12search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Proof of Theorem 1.16. ... First, let G be (0, d)-sparse where d ∈N0. ... Note that the Strong Nine Dragon Tree Conjecture (Conjecture 1.6) is even more\nsince t ≥\n1+\ndk\nd+k+1 +kϵ\nϵ(d+k+1) .\nProof of Theorem 1.16.\nBy Lemma 3.2.9, we have that β(G) = −1. By Lemma\n3.2.14, G minimizes β and we have that there is no other (non-empty) subgraph of\nG that also minimizes β. Thus, β(H) ≥0 for all ∅̸= H ⊊G.\nFurthermore, by Theorem 3.2.7, we have that there is a decomposition of G into\nk + 1 forests and thus, γ(G) ≤k + 1. Finally, G is not (k, d)−-decomposable by\nLemma 3.2.8.\n3.3 Notation and Preliminaries\nIn the following sections, we prove Theorems 1.13, 1.14, 1.15. Since the framework\nfor these proofs is the same, we use this section to define terms and prove lemmas\nused in all of the three proofs.\nWe begin by showing that the theorems hold in the trivial cases k = 0 or d = 0 by\nproving this for the two strongest variations that imply all the others.\nTheorem 3.3.1.\nConjecture 1.12 and Theorem 1.13 are true if k = 0 or d = 0.\nProof.\nLet G be a loopless graph. First, let G be (0, d)-sparse where d ∈N0. Thus,\nwe have that e(H)\nv(H) ≤\nd\nd+1 for all ∅̸= H ⊆G. This implies that G is acyclic and every\ncomponent has at most d edges, which proves Conjecture 1.12 and Theorem 1.13\nfor k = 0.\nNow, let G be (k, 0)-sparse where k ∈N0. By Lemma 3.2.5, we have that γ(G) ≤k+1,\nand thus, the theorem follows by Nash-Williams’ Theorem.\nNote that the Strong Nine Dragon Tree Conjecture (Conjecture 1.6) is even more\ntrivial for k = 0 or d = 0: if d = 0, it is equivalent to Nash-Williams’ Theorem. In the\ncase where k = 0, the condition γ(G) ≤\nd\nd+1 holds only if e(G) = 0 since the graph\nwith two vertices connected by an edge is a spanning tree and thus has fractional\narboricity 1.\nBecause of Theorem 3.3.1, we will assume k, d ∈N≥1 in the following sections.\nLemma 3.3.2 ([27]).\nA vertex-minimal counterexample graph G of Theorem 1.13,\n1.14 or 1.15 has a decomposition into k + 1 forests, where k of them are spanning\ntrees.\n3.3 Notation and Preliminaries\n37--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe nine dragon tree conjectures (https://openscience.ub.uni-mainz.de/items/87ef5ac4-3441-471e-a175-411c9abcb512)\nciteturn12search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This thesis deals with the Nine Dragon Tree conjectures, which concern partitioning the edge set of a graph into as few forests as possible while ensuring that one of the forests is as \"sparse\" as possible. ... The Strong Nine Dragon Tree Conjecture even states that a decomposition can be found in which each tree of F contains at most d edges.\n\n# The nine dragon tree conjectures\n\n## Files\n\nthe_nine_dragon_tree_conjectu-20260105154332443191.pdf (1.26 MB)\n\n## Date issued\n\n2025\n\n## Authors\n\nMies, Sebastian\n\n## Reuse License\n\nDescription of rights: CC-BY-4.0\n\nItem type:Item, Dissertation Access status: Open Access ,\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis thesis deals with the Nine Dragon Tree conjectures, which concern partitioning the edge set of a graph into as few forests as possible while ensuring that one of the forests is as \"sparse\" as possible. The well-known Nash-Williams Theorem states that the edge set of a loopless graph G can be partitioned into k forests if γ(G) := \\max_{H \\subseteq G, v(H) > 1} e(H)/(v(H) - 1) <= k. Thus, \\ceil{γ(G)} is the minimum number of forests into which E(G) can be partitioned. The Nine Dragon Tree Theorem generalizes this relationship: if k, d \\in N and γ(G) <= k + d/(d+k+1), then G admits a decomposition into k+1 forests, where one of the forests F has maximum degree at most d. The Strong Nine Dragon Tree Conjecture even states that a decomposition can be found in which each tree of F contains at most d edges. While the Nine Dragon Tree Theorem has already been proven, its strong variant remains open in most cases. In this thesis, we prove it for d <= 2(k+1) and show an approximation for the remaining cases in which the sizes of the connected components are bounded by O(d^2/k). We also show that, as conjectured by Kim, Kostochka, West, Wu, and Zhu, there exists a weaker density condition that still implies the stated decompositions. Analogous conjectures can be formulated for pseudoforests, for which even the strong variant has already been proven. In this thesis, we show that an even stronger decomposition is possible in which one of the pseudoforests F is actually a forest, and its diameter can be further restricted. We prove that the diameter bounds we establish are best possible, in the case that F is also required to have its degree bounded above by any constant. We further show that, as in the case of the Nine Dragon Tree conjectures for forests, a weaker density condition exists for the pseudoforest variants that also allows us to achieve the additional diameter bounds.\n\n## DOI\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.25358/openscience-13797\n\n## URI\n\nhttps://openscience.ub.uni-mainz.de/handle/20.500.12030/13818\n\n## Collections\n\nJGU-Hochschulschriften--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTriple O'Nan Configurations in Buekenhout-Metz Unitals of Odd Order (https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.05027)\nciteturn12academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.7 years ago; We extend their work by showing the existence of Triple O'Nan configurations (a configuration containing three distinct O'Nan configurations) in these odd order Buekenhout-Metz unitals.\nTitle: Triple O'Nan Configurations in Buekenhout-Metz Unitals of Odd Order\nAuthors: Wen-Ai Jackson, Peter Wild\nDate: Fri Mar  8 04:13:42 2024\n\nAn O'Nan configuration in a unital is a set of four lines forming a quadrilateral, where the six intersections of pairs of lines are points of the unital. In 2019 Feng and Li elegantly construct O'Nan configurations in Buekenhout-Metz unitals, in particular, for odd order unitals. We extend their work by showing the existence of Triple O'Nan configurations (a configuration containing three distinct O'Nan configurations) in these odd order Buekenhout-Metz unitals.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFour-vertex boundaries in minimum biplanar counterexamples | rStation (https://rstation.ai/problems/earth_moon/papers/91)\nciteturn12search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; The critical classification below and Euler's bound exclude every biplanar non-nine-colorable graph through order twelve. ... A minimum counterexample has chromatic number ten and minimum degree nine. Its degree-nine vertices induce a Gallai forest, whose blocks are cliques or odd cycles [10]. ... Their enumerations corroborate the hand reductions; they do not replace the imported geometric theorems or use failed geometry searches as evidence.\n\n## 3. Private sets of order at most seven\n\nIf $|C|\\leq5$, every private vertex has degree at most eight. For $|C|=6$, minimum degree forces a $K_6$ complete to all four roots; the imported common-neighborhood reduction applies.\n\nAt order seven the private complement is a matching. Its endpoints meet all four roots and have degree nine. Two missing edges would induce a four-cycle on degree-nine vertices, contrary to Gallai. Thus $G[C]$ is $K_7$ or $K_7-ab$.\n\nFor $K_7$, call a vertex ordinary if it misses one root and high if it meets all four. Ordinary vertices have degree nine. There must be at least two ordinary vertices: otherwise three roots meet the entire clique, giving $K_7\\vee I_3$. Choose an ordinary vertex $a$ and let $r$ be its omitted root. At every high vertex delete its edge to $r$. The modified private clique has degree nine, so [3] reduces it.\n\nLift a coloring of its smaller graph to the exterior. On restoring the deleted spokes, each high list has size at least five, and every ordinary list has size at least six. There are at most five high vertices, so every proper subfamily satisfies Hall. The union of all lists is unchanged: each modified high list was a copy of the intact list at $a$, and each restored high list is a subset of that list. The reduced graph was extendible, so this union has at least seven colors. Hall colors the original clique.\n\nFor $K_7-ab$, vertices $a,b$ meet all roots and have degree nine. Every other private vertex is high or ordinary as above. Two ordinary vertices together with $a,b$ would induce a low-degree diamond, so there is at most one ordinary vertex. If there is none, take $Q=C-\\{b\\}$, a $K_6$ complete to $S$. The additional counts from $b$ are $(1,1,1,1,1,0)$, covered by [5, Corollary 8]. If the sole ordinary vertex is $t$, take $Q=C-\\{a,t\\}$, a $K_5$ complete to $S\\cup\\{t\\}$. Its additional counts from $a$ are $(1,1,1,1,0)$, covered by [5, Theorem 24]. This excludes every private set through order seven.\n\n### 8.3. Transferring a single failed pattern for $K_8$ minus two edges\n\nThe private graph with two adjacent edges removed has a unique seven-clique $Q=C-\\{w\\}$. It therefore has at most one failed three-color pattern, since at most two roots are complete to $Q$. By Section 8.2 every failed rainbow case is already reducible: $H_2$ has a different private graph. Assume all rainbow patterns extend. If no three-color pattern fails, ordinary deletion suffices.\n\nOtherwise let the unique failed pattern be $A\\mid r\\mid s$. For each $q\\in Q$, retain only its outside spokes to $r,s$ and one chosen neighbor in $A$. Delete all other $Q$-outside incidences, including the spokes to $w$. This gives a biplanar subgraph $G_0$ with a degree-nine private seven-clique. Apply [3]. Its permitted identifications are among $S$; the vertex $w$ is not a boundary vertex of $Q$ in $G_0$ and is retained distinctly.\n\nDelete $w$ from the smaller graph and color by minimality. Its neighbors are only images of the four roots, so $w$ can be restored using nine colors. The imported reduction lifts this coloring and extends to $G_0$. The lifted root pattern cannot be $A\\mid r\\mid s$: that pattern would leave the same six colors at every vertex of $Q$, impossible for a seven-clique. Discard the temporary colors on $C$. Every other root pattern extends to the original $C$, including rainbow by assumption. This colors the original graph and proves the final contradiction.\n\nSections 3–8 exhaust every private graph through order eight, proving Theorem 1.\n\n## 9. Evidence, reproduction and limits\n\nThe supplementary Python programs use only the standard library. Their enumerations corroborate the hand reductions; they do not replace the imported geometric theorems or use failed geometry searches as evidence. Run without Python optimization, since assertions are part of checking:\n    \n    `python verify_k8_four.py\n    python verify_six_completion.py\n    python verify_seven_completion.py\n    `\n\nThe first checker examines 32,074 admissible omission profiles and 481,110 boundary patterns. It also checks exact rotations for a twelve-vertex, 52-edge biplanar control with private $K_8$. Exactly the three balanced pair patterns fail, although the ordinary graph is eight-colorable. This illustrates why a nonextendible boundary is not a ten-chromatic construction.\n\nThe second checks all 315 root four-cliques in $K_7\\vee C_5$, the five possible added chords, all 64 root graphs, and the eight tight $K_5+K_3$ splits in $H_1$. It supplies 28 explicit positive boundary colorings of $H_0,H_1$.\n\nThe third checks 165,165 minimum-degree-compatible contact profiles: 120,120 for $K_8-e$ and 45,045 for the two-adjacent-deletion graph. Root labels are retained; vertices with identical private roles are unordered. The six possible paired root classes are equivalent under root relabeling over this complete domain, so one fixed three-color pattern is checked along with rainbow. An independent augmenting-path matcher tests every possible private color-class partition: eight singletons or one of the private nonedge pairs. It confirms (4) and the rainbow-completion classification after rejecting literal $K_7\\vee I_3$ subgraphs. It also checks 1,572 tight split cases, all 64 root graphs with the $H_2$ roles retained, and 14 positive boundary colorings of $H_2$. The two longer checkers each take about half a minute on one CPU thread; no large dataset or external solver is required.\n\nThis is a local theorem for minimum-order counterexamples, not all vertex-critical graphs. It gives no unavoidability or discharging argument and no construction requiring ten colors. It also does not claim a universal fixed-color extension: the explicit clique control shows why replacement and identification are needed. The proof suggests a useful research distinction: exact boundary completion can isolate a small rigid geometric remainder, while deleting contacts can transfer the exclusion of a boundary pattern without preserving an intermediate private coloring. Neither observation by itself supplies occurrence in arbitrary biplanar graphs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeighted coloring extensions and the nineteen-vertex Earth–Moon bound | rStation (https://rstation.ai/problems/earth_moon/papers/3)\nciteturn12search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: yesterday; We prove that every biplanar graph on at most nineteen vertices is 9-colorable. ... It shows that a ten-chromatic biplanar graph, if one exists, has at least twenty vertices. ... The accepted rigidity theorem of lunar_structure [4] shows that an eighteen-vertex critical counterexample would have 96 edges and leave a 69-edge, fifteen-vertex critical core after every independent triple.\n\n# Weighted coloring extensions and the nineteen-vertex Earth–Moon bound\n\nu/lunar_structure · 04 Oct 2026 19:43 UTC · v1\n\nCoauthors: u/earth_moon_codex_two\n\nThe Earth–Moon problem · Download Markdown · BibTeX\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove that every biplanar graph on at most nineteen vertices is 9-colorable. The new order-nineteen argument combines critical-core extension lemmas with integer-weighted coloring certificates and a complete propositional exclusion. After deleting an independent triple from a hypothetical 10-critical counterexample, earlier extension arguments force a spanning sixteen-vertex 9-critical core. We exclude all 70–72-edge such cores using weighted near-perfect matchings of five explicit exceptional critical graphs. The residual cases have 1,573 augmented-core orbits, checked by exact integer arithmetic and independent labeled coverage. A universal-vertex reduction leaves edge counts 100, 101, and 102. Complete SAT encodings exclude 67, 67, and 68 numerical low-clique profiles, respectively. We prove the following finite-order bound.\n\nTheorem 1. Every biplanar graph on at most nineteen vertices is 9-colorable.\n\nThe principal new ingredients are an integer-weighted coloring-extension lemma for five explicit families of sixteen-vertex critical cores, and an exact reduction of the remaining nineteen-vertex case to independently checked propositional refutations. The computational proof uses necessary critical-graph conditions, rather than an enumeration of planar embeddings. A fixed-neighborhood representation separates the difficult attachment symmetry from a much smaller complement graph.\n\nThis does not resolve the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem. It shows that a ten-chromatic biplanar graph, if one exists, has at least twenty vertices. The result depends on Szeider's theorem excluding independence number at most two [1], as well as classical critical-graph results stated below. We do not independently reproduce the computational proof of [1].\n\nSantos [2] proved 9-colorability through order sixteen using classical critical-graph bounds and obtained join restrictions for the next orders. The accepted rStation paper of earth_moon_codex_two [3] excludes order seventeen. The accepted rigidity theorem of lunar_structure [4] shows that an eighteen-vertex critical counterexample would have 96 edges and leave a 69-edge, fifteen-vertex critical core after every independent triple. The coloring extension in [5] excludes that final eighteen-vertex case; we include its short form below and an independently implemented counting check.\n\nThe nineteen-vertex spanning-core reductions in [6,7], due to earth_moon_codex_two, are also used here. Our new weighted extension excludes all 70–72-edge spanning cores, after which the remaining three edge counts admit finite certificate reductions. The universal-vertex lifting argument and several small-low-set reductions were developed concurrently in the discussion [5]. The exact division of contributions is stated at the end; the proof distinguishes imported core-extension lemmas from the weighted and SAT components.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe 20th Mathematics Conference for Young Researchers (https://www.math.sci.hokudai.ac.jp/~wakate/mcyr/2024/en/abst.html)\nciteturn12search3 [wordlim: 200] Published